WEBVTT

00:04.662 --> 00:07.812
This case study comes from an MSHA report titled

00:07.812 --> 00:12.237
Powered Haulage Accident, dated September 12, 2023.

00:12.237 --> 00:14.575
It happened at a surface limestone operation in

00:14.575 --> 00:17.700
Indiana. Powered haulage, meaning trucks,

00:17.700 --> 00:20.525
loaders and conveyors, is one of the leading causes

00:20.525 --> 00:23.488
of mining deaths, and backing to a dump point is

00:23.488 --> 00:26.375
one of the most common tasks a truck driver does.

00:27.505 --> 00:29.680
The driver was an experienced miner.

00:29.680 --> 00:32.692
He was hauling material from a pit to a spoil pile,

00:32.692 --> 00:35.380
a dump site for waste rock, with a few other trucks

00:35.380 --> 00:36.680
doing the same run.

00:37.806 --> 00:40.281
In the afternoon, he backed his haul truck up to

00:40.281 --> 00:42.631
the edge of the spoil pile to dump.

00:42.631 --> 00:44.606
The truck traveled through the berm,

00:44.606 --> 00:47.294
went down the slope, and came to rest in a pond

00:47.294 --> 00:50.031
at the bottom. It was completely under water.

00:50.031 --> 00:52.644
Another driver saw dust and rocks and the truck

00:52.644 --> 00:55.618
going down the pile, and called it in on the radio.

00:56.736 --> 00:58.949
Investigators found that the driver was wearing

00:58.949 --> 01:02.074
his seat belt. It wasn't enough. When a truck goes

01:02.074 --> 01:04.986
over an edge into water, a seat belt can't save

01:04.986 --> 01:07.698
you. Only the berm and the way the dump is set

01:07.698 --> 01:08.599
up can.

01:11.914 --> 01:14.414
After the accident, investigators measured the

01:14.414 --> 01:17.126
berm. It ranged from twenty-one to thirty-eight

01:17.126 --> 01:20.489
inches high. The truck's mid-axle height was fifty-two

01:20.489 --> 01:23.451
inches. In other words, the berm was lower than

01:23.451 --> 01:26.101
the middle of the truck's wheel, so a backing truck

01:26.101 --> 01:27.876
could ride up and over it.

01:28.996 --> 01:31.121
The berm was also built from loose,

01:31.121 --> 01:34.271
unconsolidated material, with a narrow top.

01:34.271 --> 01:36.871
And trucks routinely backed into the berm to find

01:36.871 --> 01:40.008
the edge. Every bump wore it down a little more.

01:41.146 --> 01:43.709
The second root cause was the workplace exam.

01:43.709 --> 01:46.284
According to the report, the dump was examined

01:46.284 --> 01:49.496
from inside a vehicle, records weren't being kept,

01:49.496 --> 01:51.896
and the examiner hadn't been trained to recognize

01:51.896 --> 01:54.521
whether a berm was adequate. A berm that's too

01:54.521 --> 01:57.334
low or too soft is easy to miss when you're driving

01:57.334 --> 01:58.234
past.

01:59.368 --> 02:01.668
A good dump exam is done from a place where you

02:01.668 --> 02:04.468
can actually see the berm and the edge.

02:04.468 --> 02:06.706
That usually means getting out of the vehicle and

02:06.706 --> 02:09.331
walking it, staying back from the crest.

02:09.331 --> 02:11.506
The examiner checks the height against the trucks

02:11.506 --> 02:14.093
that dump there, looks for low spots,

02:14.093 --> 02:16.631
gaps and soft material, and looks at the slope

02:16.631 --> 02:19.481
below for cracks. Then it goes on the record,

02:19.481 --> 02:21.206
along with any fix.

02:22.330 --> 02:25.305
MSHA's report lists two root causes.

02:25.305 --> 02:27.593
The mine operator didn't make sure the dump berm

02:27.593 --> 02:29.805
would stop a truck from overtraveling,

02:29.805 --> 02:32.317
and didn't do an adequate workplace exam.

02:32.317 --> 02:34.718
The mine retrained its miners on building dump

02:34.718 --> 02:37.468
berms to at least mid-axle height, and changed

02:37.468 --> 02:40.367
how the dump site is examined so the examiner can

02:40.367 --> 02:41.792
actually see the berm.

02:45.129 --> 02:48.391
Two standards cover berms. On roads with a drop-off

02:48.391 --> 02:51.778
big enough to overturn equipment, berms or guardrails

02:51.778 --> 02:54.591
must be at least mid-axle height of the largest

02:54.591 --> 02:57.203
self-propelled equipment that usually uses that

02:57.203 --> 03:00.341
road. At dump points where a truck could overtravel

03:00.341 --> 03:02.828
and overturn, there must be a berm,

03:02.828 --> 03:05.728
bumper block, or similar device that actually stops

03:05.728 --> 03:06.291
it.

03:07.406 --> 03:10.393
A dump berm is not a wall to back into hard.

03:10.393 --> 03:12.543
It's there to tell you where the edge is and to

03:12.543 --> 03:14.918
stop you if something goes wrong. If you have to

03:14.918 --> 03:17.331
hit it to find it, the setup is wrong.

03:18.476 --> 03:20.714
Backing is where drivers see the least.

03:20.714 --> 03:23.401
You learned about blind spots in Module 4.

03:23.401 --> 03:25.914
A haul truck can hide a pickup, a person,

03:25.914 --> 03:28.251
or the edge of a dump behind its body.

03:28.251 --> 03:30.826
Your backup alarm must work, and you check it on

03:30.826 --> 03:33.451
your pre-shift. If a spotter is used,

03:33.451 --> 03:35.564
the spotter stays where you can see them the whole

03:35.564 --> 03:38.064
time, and you stop the moment you lose sight of

03:38.064 --> 03:38.651
them.

03:39.770 --> 03:41.795
So what does a berm that works look like?

03:41.795 --> 03:44.245
It's built from material that holds together,

03:44.245 --> 03:47.445
not loose fines that crumble. It has a wide base,

03:47.445 --> 03:50.157
so a tire pushes against the bulk of the pile and

03:50.157 --> 03:52.920
not just a thin ridge on top. It's at least as

03:52.920 --> 03:54.907
high as the middle of the wheel of the biggest

03:54.907 --> 03:57.795
truck that dumps there. And it runs the whole length

03:57.795 --> 04:00.620
of the edge, with no gaps or low spots where a

04:00.620 --> 04:01.920
truck has clipped it.

04:03.044 --> 04:06.081
A berm is only as good as its weakest spot.

04:06.081 --> 04:08.531
One low section is all it takes. That's why the

04:08.531 --> 04:11.619
examiner walks the edge, and why every driver looks

04:11.619 --> 04:13.231
at it before backing up.

04:16.555 --> 04:18.630
Here's how this lines up with our sites.

04:18.630 --> 04:21.279
Haul trucks and tri-axle road trucks both haul

04:21.279 --> 04:23.842
inside the pit at every Allen site,

04:23.842 --> 04:26.680
so this applies to both. Traffic keeps right,

04:26.680 --> 04:28.542
and haul trucks have the right of way.

04:28.542 --> 04:31.567
Speed limits are fifteen miles per hour on site,

04:31.567 --> 04:34.142
and at Boonesboro, twenty-five underground.

04:34.142 --> 04:36.667
If you're in a pickup, radio the haul truck before

04:36.667 --> 04:39.042
you approach it, and get an answer back.

04:40.176 --> 04:42.551
And every site has water, like pumps,

04:42.551 --> 04:45.251
sumps and ponds. Where there's a danger of falling

04:45.251 --> 04:48.226
into water, a life jacket is required.

04:49.338 --> 04:51.401
Our own records point the same way.

04:51.401 --> 04:54.601
At Dix River, inspectors cited missing berms and

04:54.601 --> 04:58.075
unsafe access. At Clover Bottom, they cited horns

04:58.075 --> 05:00.250
and backup alarms that didn't work.

05:00.250 --> 05:03.563
And at Boonesboro in 2022, a miner riding on the

05:03.563 --> 05:06.063
outside of a loader got his leg caught when it

05:06.063 --> 05:08.688
turned. None of these are rare events.

05:08.688 --> 05:10.851
They're the everyday conditions that let haulage

05:10.851 --> 05:12.113
accidents happen.

05:15.436 --> 05:17.711
If you drive, look at the dump berm before you

05:17.711 --> 05:20.411
back up to it. Is it at least as high as the middle

05:20.411 --> 05:23.061
of your wheel? Is it solid, or is it loose and

05:23.061 --> 05:26.274
worn? Back slowly and straight, stop short,

05:26.274 --> 05:28.361
and don't use the berm as a bumper.

05:28.361 --> 05:31.024
If you're unsure about the edge, dump short and

05:31.024 --> 05:33.461
let the loader or dozer push it over.

05:33.461 --> 05:37.036
And report a low, soft or damaged berm right away.

05:38.158 --> 05:40.408
Stay in your cab while you're being loaded.

05:40.408 --> 05:42.671
That's the rule at every Allen site.

05:42.671 --> 05:45.358
And always wear your seat belt, even though,

05:45.358 --> 05:48.120
as this report shows, the berm is what keeps you

05:48.120 --> 05:49.220
out of the water.

05:50.344 --> 05:53.094
If you're on foot or in a pickup, never walk or

05:53.094 --> 05:55.869
drive behind a backing truck. Make contact with

05:55.869 --> 05:58.894
the driver and get an answer back before you approach

05:58.894 --> 06:01.881
any mobile equipment. Stay out of the blind spots,

06:01.881 --> 06:04.106
and never assume the driver can see you.

06:05.218 --> 06:08.268
Dump points change fast. Rain can soften the edge

06:08.268 --> 06:10.930
and wash out a berm. Each load dumped over the

06:10.930 --> 06:13.955
edge changes the slope, and cracks can open behind

06:13.955 --> 06:17.130
the crest. If you see cracks, soft ground,

06:17.130 --> 06:19.830
or the edge sloughing, don't back up to it.

06:19.830 --> 06:23.118
Stop, dump somewhere else as your supervisor directs,

06:23.118 --> 06:25.781
and report it so the edge can be pulled back and

06:25.781 --> 06:27.005
the berm rebuilt.

06:28.144 --> 06:31.082
To sum up. A berm that's lower than mid-axle,

06:31.082 --> 06:34.132
or built from loose material, won't stop a truck.

06:34.132 --> 06:36.732
Don't use the berm as a bumper. Dump sites get

06:36.732 --> 06:39.907
a real exam every shift, not a glance from a moving

06:39.907 --> 06:43.369
vehicle. Make contact before you approach any truck.

06:43.369 --> 06:46.182
And if a berm looks wrong, report it before anyone

06:46.182 --> 06:47.419
backs up to it.

06:48.550 --> 06:51.712
Remember: your site's plans and procedures always

06:51.712 --> 06:53.212
override this video.
